{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 33, :comments ["<b>That which falls from them.</b> 'In one of them' — as in 'וייקבר בערי הגלעד באחת מערי' (in one of the cities). <b>Into its interior:</b> it does not say 'into it' but 'into its interior' (אל תוכו). Rashi explained: an earthen vessel contracts impurity only from its inside airspace. The reason: since it has no purification except by breaking, the Torah was lenient regarding its loss, so that it does not become impure from its outside, as it is written: 'וכל כלי פתוח אשר אין צמיד פתיל עליו טמא הוא' — the implication: if it has a tightly sealed cover, it is pure. For if it contracted impurity from its outside too, it would constantly require breaking and then there would be no remedy for it." "<b>And it you shall break.</b> Because it draws impurity more than other vessels, and further, its value is slight and there is no monetary loss but a small amount."], :book :leviticus, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {0 "The comment quotes Rashi by name. The phrase 'אל תוכו' is the lemma from the verse, introduced mid-comment as a new sub-lemma."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:11:36.469724893Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}